rust.yaml declared seven ASSOCIATED FUNCTIONS as methods — File.open /
create / create_new, TcpStream.connect, TcpListener.bind, UdpSocket.bind,
Command.new. `File::open(path)` takes no receiver, so its call site can
only produce a function-construct edge, and the cost was not a
mislabelled finding:

  (a) io_boundary's method-kind gate could never match it, so the whole
      std::fs::File half of the fs catalogue was unreachable BY
      CONSTRUCTION; and
  (b) method_starved_modules saw a resolved call into a method-keyed
      module with no method-construct edge, concluded the analysis "did
      not look", and withheld the verdict.

Measured on encrypted-dns-server (rust+toml only): the edge resolves
perfectly — rust:std::fs::File:0-0:open:external_symbol, module and name
both correct — and all 7 generic claims returned inconclusive naming
std::fs::File structurally invisible. Re-kinding std::fs::File alone
dropped the starved list from two modules to one on the same binary.

The correction is a SPLIT, not a move: TcpStream::connect is an
associated function while TcpStream::write_all is a method under the same
module, and OpenOptions legitimately carries both, so it is untouched.
Controls for the method half ship beside the subjects.

python.yaml carried the same error twice: pathlib.Path.cwd and .home are
classmethods, verified with inspect.getattr_static rather than recall.

